      "id": 1,
      "text": "Alpaugh takes its name from John Alpaugh, an officer of the Home Extension Colony that reclaimed the land the town sits on.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alpaugh%2C_California",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town lies on the historic shoreline of Tulare Lake, once the largest freshwater lake in the United States outside the Great Lakes.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alpaugh%2C_California",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Alpaugh is served by the West Isle Line, a private railroad owned by Western Farm Services.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alpaugh%2C_California",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Founded in 1981, the Alpaugh Historical Society operates a museum and archives in the historic Community Center, preserving local heritage.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
